## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests the candidate's ability to design a scalable, power-efficient timer subsystem that can handle high-frequency timer events across multiple CPUs while maintaining sub-millisecond wakeup latencies.

## Key Concepts
- **Tickless operation**: Disabling periodic timer ticks during idle periods to reduce power consumption
- **Per-CPU timer buckets**: Local deadline queues that minimize cross-CPU synchronization overhead
- **Global min-heap**: Centralized data structure for tracking the nearest timer expiration across all CPUs
- **Memory ordering**: Using memory barriers to ensure proper visibility of timer state between CPUs
- **Atomic operations**: CAS for lock-free timer insertion and deletion
- **Latency bounds**: Worst-case wakeup latency is determined by hardware interrupt latency plus data structure traversal time

## Code Example
```c
struct timer {
    uint64_t expiry;
    void (*cb)(void*);
    void *arg;
    struct timer *next;
};

typedef struct {
    struct timer *head;        // Sorted by expiry (earliest at head)
    uint64_t next_expiry;     // Cached minimum for quick comparison
} per_cpu_timer_t;

per_cpu_timer_t *timer_buckets[NUM_CPUS];
min_heap_t global_timer_heap;  // Tracks minimum expiry across all CPUs

void schedule_timer(int cpu, uint64_t delta, void (*cb)(void*), void *arg);
void timer_expired(void);
```

